++For an owner with m scored submissions and best single-submission tail probability p, we com-
+pute the probability of observing at least one equally extreme result under independent, identically
+calibrated submission scores:
+
+                                        padj = 1 − (1 − p)m .
+
+This is the independent-submission selection adjustment. It grows with the number of opportuni-
+ties to obtain a high score. For independent counts obeying the stated binomial null, it is exact at
+attainable score thresholds; with merely valid discrete p-values it is conservative. We do not fit a
+distribution of latent owner skill. The calculation is fully determined by the best score, the track
+denominator and the visible scored-submission count.
+We apply the Benjamini–Hochberg step-up rule separately within Small and Main at false-discovery-
+rate target 0.05. Sorting the adjusted p-values across the M scored owners, the rule retains the largest
+rank r satisfying the threshold below and rejects the preceding ranks. Bonferroni at level 0.05 is
+reported as a sensitivity check. [9]
+
+                                          padj,(r) ≤ q r/M.
