+3      Exact power and an actionable item budget
+3.1    Single-submission planning calculation
+
+We first plan for a single pre-specified submission without owner selection or an across-owner cor-
+rection. For each candidate sample size N, the rejection threshold c(N) is the smallest integer correct
+count whose exact fair-coin upper tail is at most 0.05. Under an alternative accuracy a with indepen-
+dent items, power is the probability of reaching that count:
+
+             c(N ) = min{k : p(k, N ) ≤ α},        π(N, a) =          Pr     {K ≥ c(N )}.
+                                                               K∼Bin(N,a)
+
+If no count satisfies the level constraint, power is zero. Using the exact integer threshold preserves
+the conservative behavior of a discrete test. It also makes power non-monotone in N near the target:
+adding an item sometimes increases the required correct count. A normal approximation or a single
+first-crossing value hides this sawtooth behavior.
+
+Table 2: Exact single-submission binomial power on the inferred validation grids. This calculation
+precedes selection and multiplicity correction.
+
+Grid                 Rejection count      Actual null          Power at             Power at
+                                          rejection            accuracy 0.6         accuracy 0.65
+                                          probability
+Small, N = 14        11                   0.0287               0.12                 0.22
+Main, N = 21         15                   0.0392               0.2                  0.36
+
+At true accuracy 0.6, the single-submission test has power 0.12 on the Small grid and 0.2 on Main.
+At 0.65, these rise to 0.22 and 0.36. These probabilities describe repeated independent evaluations
+under the model; they are not confidence levels for any named submission. They explain why a
+moderate gain can remain unresolved on a tiny leaderboard even before correcting a best-of-many
+search.
+
+3.2    First crossing and sustained power
+
+We report both the first N reaching target power 0.8 and the first N after which every larger sample
+size stays at or above that target. The latter is a more robust planning recommendation. The ex-
+ecutable search certifies the sustained threshold by exact enumeration followed by an analytic tail
+bound for all larger N, rather than treating a finite search horizon as proof of a permanent crossing.
+
+Table 3: First crossing and sustained target power under independent-item planning. Sustained
+thresholds are recommended for item budgeting.
+
+True accuracy                      First N with target power           Sustained N with target power
+0.6                                158                                 169
+0.65                               69                                  76
+
+The first crossings are 69–158 items for the stated alternatives. We recommend the sustained values,
+at least 76 items to detect accuracy 0.65 or 169 to detect 0.6, under independent-item planning. A
+nearby larger sample should not be assumed adequate from the first crossing alone. The released
+tool computes exact power at each candidate sample size and records the threshold certification, so
+the calculation can be inspected without reinterpreting rounded table entries.
+These are lower-complexity planning scenarios than the observed leaderboard analysis. They test
+one method against a fixed reference; they do not guarantee power after owner selection, false-
+discovery correction, pairwise comparisons or model development on validation feedback. A final
